.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt CONCHA-TUTORIAL 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm concha-tutorial
.Nd a tour of concha for people who already use a Unix shell
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This tour assumes you already use a Unix shell.
The complete reference is
.Xr concha 1 .
.Sh 1. COMMANDS WORK AS BEFORE
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% ls -l /etc | grep conf | wc -l
% make 2> errors.txt && ./prog || echo failed
% sleep 10 &
% cat < in.txt > out.txt
.Ed
.Pp
Pipes, redirections,
.Cm && , ||
and
.Cm &
mean what they mean in
.Xr sh 1 .
Quote with single quotes, Pascal style:
.Cm 'it''s' .
Double quotes interpolate:
.Cm "\(dqhello $USER\(dq" .
.Sh 2. ANYTHING MORE IS PASCAL
A line that starts with a keyword,
.Cm "name :="
or
.Cm "name("
is Pascal:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% n := 3
% writeln('n squared is ', n * n)
n squared is 9
% for i := 1 to n do echo step $i
step 1
step 2
step 3
.Ed
.Pp
Semicolons between statements are optional, and a newline ends a
command.
Blocks look like Pascal:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if exists('build') then begin
  echo cleaning
  rm -r build
end else
  echo nothing to clean
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm else , end
and
.Cm until
end a command when they close an open
.Cm if , begin , case
or
.Cm repeat ,
so a command can be the body of an
.Cm if .
Anywhere else they are plain words.
Inside a block, write
.Cm "echo 'end'"
to print the word itself.
.Sh 3. VALUES STAY WHOLE
In
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Cm $x
is re-split on blanks and then globbed.
In
.Nm concha ,
.Cm $x
is one argument.
It becomes several arguments only when
.Ar x
holds a list:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% f := 'my file.txt'
% touch $f                     // one file, with a space in its name
% files := ['a.txt', 'b.txt']
% rm $files                    // two arguments
.Ed
.Pp
Command output is a string.
To split it, say how:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
for f in lines(`git ls-files`) do wc -l $f
words := split('a b  c')       // ['a', 'b', 'c']
fields := split('x:y:z', ':')
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm $( Ns Ar expr Ns Cm \&)
puts any expression into a word:
.Cm "echo $(n * 2) files" ,
.Cm "cp $f $(f + '.bak')" .
.Pp
Globs work on literal text only.
A glob that matches nothing is an error, not a silent literal, so
write
.Cm "find . -name '*.o'"
with quotes.
.Fn glob "'*.o'"
returns a list that may be empty.
.Sh 4. STATUS IS A VARIABLE
Every command sets
.Ev status .
Conditions in
.Nm concha
are real booleans, so you test the variable:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
grep -q TODO notes.txt
if status = 0 then echo 'still work to do'
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm &&
and
.Cm ||
still work, and are shorter for one-liners.
.Sh 5. PROCEDURES ARE COMMANDS
.Bd -literal -offset indent
procedure mkcd(dir);
begin
  mkdir -p $dir && cd $dir
end;

mkcd /tmp/scratch              // called like a command
mkcd('/tmp/scratch')           // or like Pascal
.Ed
.Pp
A procedure gets its parameters plus the whole argument list in
.Ev args :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
procedure ll; begin ls -lh $args end;
ll /etc /tmp
.Ed
.Pp
Functions return
.Ev result :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
function newest(pattern);
var best, f;
begin
  best := '';
  for f in glob(pattern) do
    if (best = '') or (int(`stat -c %Y $f`) > int(`stat -c %Y $best`)) then
      best := f;
  result := best
end;

vi $(newest('*.log'))
.Ed
.Pp
Variables you create in a procedure are local.
Globals can be read and assigned.
.Pp
A comment just above a procedure is its help:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
// make dir and go into it
procedure mkcd(dir); ...

help mkcd      // its heading, its file and line, the comment
type ls        // what a name is: procedure, builtin, or programs on PATH
help           // every builtin, and how it is used
.Ed
.Sh 6. FEEDING THE SHELL
The Bourne
.Cm "while read"
idiom works.
Put a
.Cm begin ... end
block where the redirection should apply:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
count := 0
begin
  while readln(line) do
    if line <> '' then inc(count)
end < data.txt
writeln(count, ' non-empty lines')
.Ed
.Pp
A block or loop can also be a pipeline stage:
.Cm "ps ax | while readln(l) do ..." .
There it runs in a child process, as it would in
.Xr sh 1 ,
so assignments inside don't survive.
Use the redirection form when you need the result.
.Sh 7. HEREDOCS INDENT WITH YOUR CODE
.Bd -literal -offset indent
procedure usage;
begin
  cat <<EOF
    usage: $prog [-v] file
      -v   verbose
    EOF
end;
.Ed
.Pp
The terminator's indentation is removed from every line, so the
output starts at column 0.
Quote the tag,
.Cm <<'EOF' ,
for literal text.
.Cm <<<'text'
feeds a single string.
.Sh 8. SCRIPTS
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env concha
if length(args) = 0 then begin
  echo 'usage: backup dir...' >&2
  halt(2)
end;
for d in args do tar czf $(d + '.tgz') $d
.Ed
.Pp
A script is parsed completely before it runs, so a syntax error on the
last line stops it before the first line does anything.
Calling an unknown routine is not a syntax error \(en a routine may be
defined later in the script, or by one it sources \(en so that is
caught only when the call itself runs.
Errors report the file and line, even inside a file the script sources,
and exit with status 2.
.Sh 9. AT THE PROMPT
The line editor uses the usual keys: arrows,
.Ic ^A , ^E , ^W , ^U , ^K
and
.Ic ^R
to search history.
As you type, the newest history line that starts with what you have
typed is shown dim after the cursor;
.Ic Right
at the end of the line takes it.
History is saved in
.Pa ~/.concha_history .
Define your prompt in
.Pa ~/.concharc :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
procedure prompt;
begin
  if status <> 0 then write(red, status, reset, ' ');
  write(PWD, ' > ')
end;
.Ed
.Pp
.Ev red , reset
and the other colour names hold terminal escapes, or nothing when
colour is off.
.Pp
.Ic Tab
completes commands, variables and file names.
If nothing starts with what you typed, names holding its letters in
order will do, so
.Ql dwn
finds
.Ql Downloads .
When several fit, they appear in a menu below the line;
.Ic Tab
again steps through them, and
.Ic Enter
takes one.
A function named
.Ic complete
teaches it the arguments of your commands: it gets the command's
words so far, the last being the one to complete, and returns what
that word might become.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
function complete(words);
begin
  result := [];
  if (words[0] = 'git') and (length(words) = 2) then
    result := ['add', 'commit', 'diff', 'log', 'status']
end;
.Ed
.Pp
When it returns nothing, the program's completion file is used, if it
has one: a few lines in
.Pa ~/.local/share/concha/complete/ Ns Ar program
naming its subcommands, options and operands, which
.Ic Tab
completes without ever running the program
.Pq see Xr concha-complete 5 .
Without one, file names are completed as usual.
.Ql "writeln(completions('git c'))"
shows what
.Ic Tab
would offer for a line.
.Pp
On a colour terminal the line is highlighted as you type: strings,
variables and keywords each have a colour, and the command word turns
blue once it names something that can run, red while it does not.
Set
.Ev NO_COLOR
to turn all colour off.
.Sh FROM SH TO CONCHA
.Bl -column "while read l; do ...; done < fXX" "begin while readln(l) do ... end < fXX"
.It Sy sh Ta Sy concha
.It Cm x=1 Ta Cm "x := 1"
.It Cm "export X=1" Ta Cm "export X := 1"
.It Cm "echo \(dq$x\(dq" Ta Cm "echo $x"
.It Cm $(cmd) Ta Cm \&`cmd\&`
.It Cm "$((a + b))" Ta Cm "$(a + b)"
.It Cm "diff <(a) <(b)" Ta Cm "diff <(a) <(b)"
.It Cm "[ -f f ]" Ta Cm "isfile('f')"
.It Cm "[ \(dq$a\(dq = \(dq$b\(dq ]" Ta Cm "a = b"
.It Cm "if cmd; then ...; fi" Ta Cm "cmd; if status = 0 then ..."
.It Cm "for f in *.c; do ...; done" Ta Cm "for f in glob('*.c') do ..."
.It Cm "while read l; do ...; done < f" Ta Cm "begin while readln(l) do ... end < f"
.It Cm "case $x in a|b) ...;; esac" Ta Cm "case x of 'a', 'b': ... end"
.It Cm "f() { ...; }" Ta Cm "procedure f; begin ... end;"
.It Cm "$1, $#, $@" Ta Cm "args[0], length(args), $args"
.It Cm $? Ta Cm status
.It Cm $$ Ta Cm pid
.It Cm $! Ta Cm lastpid
.It Cm "${x:-default}" Ta Cm "if not defined('x') then x := 'default'"
.It Cm "${#x}" Ta Cm "length(x)"
.It Cm "${x%.c}" Ta Cm "copy(x, 1, length(x) - 2)"
.It Cm "$(basename \(dq$f\(dq .c)" Ta Cm "basename(f, '.c')"
.It Cm "${x//a/b}" Ta Cm "replace(x, 'a', 'b')"
.It Cm "export \(dq$n=$v\(dq" Ta Cm "setenv(n, v)"
.It Cm "$(date +%F)" Ta Cm "time('%F')"
.It Cm "IFS=: read a b" Ta Cm "f := split(line, ':')"
.It Cm "trap 'rm $t' EXIT" Ta Cm "trap cleanup EXIT"
.It Cm "set -e" Ta Cm "errexit := true"
.It Cm "set -o pipefail" Ta Cm "pipefail := true"
.It Cm "exit 1" Ta Cm "halt(1)"
.It Cm ". file" Ta Cm "source file"
.It Cm "type -a cmd" Ta Cm "type cmd"
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Short commented scripts, each showing a few features, are installed in
.Pa /usr/local/share/concha/examples ,
or under whatever prefix concha was installed with.
Run one with
.Ql concha file ,
or read it first.
.Bl -tag -width "rename-ext.cnXX"
.It Pa hello.cn
Greets its arguments; a usage message and
.Ic halt .
.It Pa count.cn
Counts lines, words and characters like
.Xr wc 1 ,
with
.Ic readln
in a redirected block.
.It Pa rename-ext.cn
Changes file extensions in a directory, with a
.Fl n
dry run.
.It Pa wordfreq.cn
The most frequent words of a file: a sorted list piped to
.Xr uniq 1 .
.It Pa csv-sum.cn
Adds up one column of a CSV file, chosen by name or number.
.It Pa template.cn
Fills a letter from variables with indented heredocs.
.It Pa backup.cn
Dated
.Xr tar 1
archives, with
.Va errexit
and a
.Ic trap
on
.Cm EXIT
to clean up.
.It Pa bigfiles.cn
The largest files under a directory, found with
.Xr find 1
and sorted.
.It Pa parallel.cn
Checksums files in background jobs, then collects each one's status
with
.Ic wait
and
.Va lastpid .
.It Pa todo.cn
A to-do list in
.Pa ~/.todo :
.Ql todo.cn add ... ,
.Ql list
and
.Ql done Ar n .
.It Pa concharc
A commented
.Pa ~/.concharc :
a coloured prompt that counts the lines changed in a git or sauce tree,
aliases as procedures,
.Ic export
and a
.Ql mkcd .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr concha 1 ,
.Xr concha-complete 5
